%S A137314 4,28,30,54,60,70,78,94,100,108,118,126,166,196,214,238,244,268,286,
%T A137314 316,324,334,348,364,406,430,438,444,478,484,508,510,516,534,550,558,
%U A137314 574,604,606,628,660,670,684,708,748,790,796,820,838,846,886,924,948,966
%N A137314 Numbers k such that a type-7 Gaussian normal basis over GF(2^k) exists.
%C A137314 A type-t Gaussian normal basis exists for GF(2^k) if and only if p = k*t+1 is prime and gcd(k, (p-1)/ord(2 mod p)) = 1.
%C A137314 From _Jianing Song_, Oct 06 2026: (Start)
%C A137314 Note that p = k*t+1 being prime and gcd(k, (p-1)/ord(2 mod p)) = 1 imply that (p-1)/ord(2 mod p) divides t. In particular, k is a term in this sequence if and only if k satisfies one the following conditions:
%C A137314 (i) ord(2 mod p) = p-1 (k = 4, 28, 30, 54, 60, ...);
%C A137314 (ii) k is not divisible by 7, and ord(2 mod p) = (p-1)/7 (k = 166, 244, 286, 444, 484, ...). (End)
%H A137314 Jianing Song, <a href="/A137314/b137314.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A137314 Joerg Arndt, <a href="https://www.jjj.de/fxt/#fxtbook">Matters Computational (The Fxtbook)</a>, section 42.9 "Gaussian normal bases", pp. 914-920.
%o A137314 (PARI) istypet(n,t=7) = my(p); (isprime(p=n*t+1) && gcd(n, (p-1)/znorder(Mod(2, p))) == 1) \\ _Jianing Song_, Oct 06 2026, following _Michel Marcus_'s program in A101284
%Y A137314 Cf. A071642 (type-1), A054639 (type-2), A136415 (type-3), A137310 (type-4), A137311 (type-5), A137313 (type-6), A101284 (type-8).
%K A137314 nonn,changed
